[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No connectivity fault is present on the Location B access switch or the host ports. I verified end-to-end L2/L3 between the two desks:

- From PC15 (172.18.51.15) to PC16 (172.18.51.16) and vice versa, ICMP succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs (4/4 replies each direction). ARP on PC15 shows 172.18.51.16 reachable at MAC 9a:a6:92:08:62:12.
- Both endpoints have correct IPs (172.18.51.15/24 and 172.18.51.16/24), interfaces are UP/LOWER_UP, and both have the same /24 mask.
- On switch10 (Location B access switch), the Linux bridge br0 has both access ports up and learning: the FDB shows PC15’s MAC (8e:29:c9:80:69:30) on eth1 and PC16’s MAC (9a:a6:92:08:62:12) on eth2. This confirms frames are being learned and forwarded across those ports normally.

Mechanism: If the switch were dropping frames or a port were down, we would see failed pings, absent ARP resolution, and no dynamic MAC learning on the bridge FDB. Instead, all three indicators are healthy, so the reported symptom is not reproducible now.

Localization: Location B access switch (switch10) ports eth1 (PC15) and eth2 (PC16) and both PCs’ NICs.

Root cause: No network fault found; issue was likely transient or endpoint/application-specific at the time of the report.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the problem recurs, capture timestamps and run simultaneous ping/arp -n checks on both PCs; also review host firewall/AV policies at the endpoints.
=== END ===